To pull the **CNAME** records from VirusTotal you’ll hit their *DNS‑records* endpoint for the target domain and filter on `record_type`.  
Below is the exact request you’d send (replace `<YOUR_API_KEY>` with your own key – here shown as `secret123`).  

```http
GET /api/v3/domains/sample.com/dns_records?record_type=CNAME HTTP/1.1
Host: www.virustotal.com
X-Apikey: secret123          # <-- your API key
```

When this request succeeds, VirusTotal returns a JSON payload similar to:

```json
{
    "data": [
        {
            "id": "dns_record_01",
            "attributes": { … }
        },
        {
            "id": "dns_record_02",
            "attributes": { … }
        }
        …
    ]
}
```

Only the `"id"` values belong to the objects you asked for.  
So after parsing the response you would simply collect those IDs into an array, e.g.:

```python
import requests

url = "https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/domains/sample.com/dns_records"
params = {"record_type": "CNAME"}
headers = {"x-apikey": "secret123"}

resp = requests.get(url, headers=headers, params=params)
ids = [item["id"] for item in resp.json()["data"]]
print(ids)   # → ['dns_record_01', 'dns_record_02', …]
```

If you run the above against the live API, replace `'dns_record_01'`, `'dns_record_02'`, … with whatever identifiers VirusTotal actually assigns to each CNAME entry for `sample.com`.
